        ...
    }
    i++;
    left -= (zip_uint64_t)entry_size;
}
```

`_zip_dirent_read` reads from `cd_buffer`. If the entry has a very large filename, extra field, or comment, `_zip_dirent_read` would try to read more data than available in `cd_buffer`. But `_zip_dirent_read` checks `_zip_buffer_left(buffer) < variable_size` and returns an error if there's not enough data.

Wait, but `_zip_dirent_read` also reads from `src` (the zip source) when `buffer` is NULL. But in this case, `buffer` (cd_buffer) is not NULL, so it reads from the buffer.

Actually, looking at `_zip_dirent_read` more carefully:

```c
if (from_buffer) {
    if (_zip_buffer_left(buffer) < variable_size) {
        zip_error_set(error, ZIP_ER_INCONS, 0);
        return -1;
    }
}
else {
    _zip_buffer_free(buffer);
    if ((buffer = _zip_buffer_new_from_source(src, variable_size, NULL, error)) == NULL) {
        return -1;
    }
}
```

When `from_buffer` is true (which is the case when `cd_buffer` is used), it checks if there's enough data in the buffer. If not, it returns an error.

But wait - `_zip_dirent_read` also reads the filename, extra fields, and comment from the buffer:

```c
if (filename_len) {
    zde->filename = _zip_read_string(buffer, src, filename_len, 1, error);
    ...
}

if (ef_len) {
    zip_uint8_t *ef = _zip_read_data(buffer, src, ef_len, 0, error);
    ...
}

if (comment_len) {
    zde->comment = _zip_read_string(buffer, src, comment_len, 0, error);
    ...
}
```

These functions read from the buffer. If the buffer doesn't have enough data, they should return NULL (error).

